=3 => y=0 invalid. So one point. So max 2 points. So slope -3 cannot get 3 points.\n\nThus maybe only slopes 1, -2, -1/2 can achieve the bound for odd k. Let's test slope -1/2 (p=-1,q=2) we already saw works. Slope -2 (p=-2,q=1) works. Slope 1 (p=1,q=1) works. Are there any other slopes with |p+q|=2 that can achieve (k+1)/2 points? Let's test p=3,q=-1? But q>0 required, so q must be positive. So p+q=2 => p = 2 - q. For q>0 integer, p integer. gcd(p,q)=1. Let's list q=1 => p=1 => slope 1. q=2 => p=0 => p≠0 fails. q=3 => p=-1 => slope -1/3 (we tested fails). q=4 => p=-2 => slope -2/4 = -1/2 (but p and q not coprime: gcd(-2,4)=2, not allowed). q=5 => p=-3 => slope -3/5 (maybe fails). q=6 => p=-4 => gcd(4,6)=2 fails. q=7 => p=-5 => slope -5/7. So many possibilities but gcd condition restricts to p and q coprime. So slopes -1/3, -3/5, -5/7, etc. Let's test slope -3/5 for k=5. Equation: p=-3,q=5 => -3x -5y = c => 3x +5y = d. Points: 3x +5y = d. For small x,y positive, we need x+y ≤6. Let's try to find d such that we get 3 points. Let's brute mental: For y=1 => 3x+5 = d => x = (d-5)/3. For integer x, d ≡ 5 mod 3 => d = 5,8,11,... For y=2 => 3x+10 = d => x = (d-10)/3 => d ≡ 1 mod 3. For y=3 => 3x+15 = d => x = (d-15)/3 => d ≡ 0 mod 3. So d must satisfy multiple congruences for multiple points. Let's try to find d that yields three points with x+y ≤6. Let's try d=20: y=1 => x=5 (5+1=6 ok). y=2 => x= (20-10)/3 = 10/3 not integer. y=3 => x= (20-15)/3 = 5/3 not integer. So only one point. d=23: y=1 => x=6 (6+1=7 >6 invalid). y=2 => x= (23-10)/3 =13/3 not integer. y=3 => x= (23-15)/3 =8/3 not integer. So none. d=26: y=1 => x=7 (invalid). y=2 => x= (26-10)/3 =16/3 not integer. y=3 => x= (26-15)/3 =11/3 not integer. So none. d=29: y=1 => x=8 invalid. y=2 => x= (29-10)/3 =19/3 not integer. y=3 => x= (29-15)/3 =14/3 not integer. So none. So slope -3/5 cannot get many points.\n\nThus maybe indeed only slopes 1, -2, -1/2 can achieve the bound. Let's try slope -1/2 for k=7 (odd). T_7 h