nes. Actually we used n-3 vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3, plus vertical x=n-2, plus horizontal y=1 = (n-3)+2 = n-1 lines. So we have one line left, which must be a sunny line (since we need exactly 2 sunny lines total). But we have only used 1 sunny line so far (none). Actually we haven't used any sunny lines yet. So we have one line left, which could be a sunny line. But we need exactly 2 sunny lines, not 1. So we need to use two sunny lines, meaning we need to replace some non-sunny line with a sunny line. But we have constraints: column n-2 forced vertical line (non-sunny). So we cannot replace that. Horizontal y=1 is non-sunny, but maybe we could replace it with a sunny line that covers (n-1,1) and (n,1) and maybe some other points. But any line covering (n-1,1) and (n,1) is horizontal (non-sunny). So we cannot replace horizontal y=1 with a sunny line covering both. So we need to cover (n-1,1) and (n,1) with separate lines: maybe one sunny line covering (n-1,1) and some other point, and another sunny line covering (n,1) and some other point. That would use two sunny lines for those two points, and we could drop horizontal line entirely. Then we would have vertical x=n-2 (non-sunny), vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (non-sunny), and two sunny lines covering (n-1,1) and (n,1) respectively, plus maybe also covering other points. That would be total lines: (n-3) vertical + 1 vertical (x=n-2) = n-2 non-sunny lines, plus 2 sunny lines = n lines. So that matches the count. However, we need to ensure that the two sunny lines also cover all other points not yet covered. Let's see: Points in columns 1,...,n-3 are covered by vertical lines. Points in column n-2 are covered by vertical x=n-2. Points (n-1,2) and (n-1,1) and (n,1) remain. We have two sunny lines to cover these three points. Since any line covering two of them is non-sunny, each sunny line can cover at most one of these three points. So we need at least three lines to cover them if we restrict to sun